        "description": "<div id=\"link_wrapped_content\">\n<body><section class=\"wp-block-bigbite-multi-title\"><div class=\"container\"></div></section>\n\n\n\n<p>Delving into Pope Leo XIV\u2019s exhaustive treatise about humanity and AI, I was struck by a recurring theme: AI simulates fundamental human traits that it doesn\u2019t actually possess.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>For starters, AI lacks the grounding we humans get from our real-world experiences, Pope Leo noted in his first encyclical, which was released Monday by the Vatican. Yes, AI models like ChatGPT (or more specifically, GPT), Claude, and Gemini are trained on mountains of data that seemingly represent the entirety of human knowledge.\u00a0But all that data is just that: data. Billions and billions of words. They\u2019re not real-world experiences with texture, sights, sounds, smells, and meaning.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>The Pope also emphasized that AI can only simulate human empathy and morality, and that it has no true sense of consequence. AI may also come across as objective when it\u2019s actually t",
